The rate estimator now has the same kind of sampling floor as the signature
estimator. For a completed count N over elapsed time T, Poisson standard error
is sqrt(N)/T, or 1/sqrt(N) relative to its rate. Comparing two estimates adds
their variances; the controller's existing two-error convention therefore
gives 2*sqrt(2/N). The coefficient is statistical, not a machine percentage.

The monitor records actual completed counts, never commands/second as a count.
The existing equal-weight pair of rates retains the smaller nonempty
constituent's count. This conservatively handles unequal elapsed windows and
overlapping readings without pooling commands into fictitious precision.
Movement resets invalidate the previous count along with the previous rate.
An all-empty reading adds no relative-noise floor, preserving detection of a
complete stop against the existing busy anchor.

